{
  "id": "metals___latent_heat_of_melting",
  "title": "Metals - Latent Heat of Melting",
  "description": "Metals and their latent heat of fusion. The Latent Heat of Fusion is the heat supplied to a solid body at the melting point when it changes state from solid to liquid.",
  "type": "chart",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "# Latent Heat of Fusion Reference\n\nThe **Latent Heat of Fusion** is the heat supplied to a solid body at the melting point when it changes state from solid to liquid.\n\nLatent heat of fusion is calculated as:\n\n**L = 1000 × L_mol / A**\n\nWhere:\n- **L** = latent heat of fusion (kJ/kg)\n- **L_mol** = latent heat of fusion (kJ/mol)\n- **A** = atomic weight (g/mol)\n\nConversion: 1 kJ/kg = 0.23884 cal/g = 0.23884 kcal/kg = 0.4299 Btu/lbm"
    },
